1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:02,154
A server suddenly feels slow.

2
00:00:02,174 --> 00:00:05,579
Something is using all the processor, or all the memory.

3
00:00:05,599 --> 00:00:08,209
How do you find it, and how do you stop it?

4
00:00:08,679 --> 00:00:14,526
In this video: identify CPU and memory intensive processes, and kill processes.

5
00:00:19,046 --> 00:00:21,629
Here's the task, the way an exam would state it.

6
00:00:22,099 --> 00:00:26,879
Identify CPU and memory intensive processes, and kill processes.

7
00:00:27,349 --> 00:00:31,745
We'll start some busy processes, find the greediest ones, and stop them,

8
00:00:31,765 --> 00:00:34,198
politely first, and by force if we must.

9
00:00:43,718 --> 00:00:48,448
A process is a running program. Every process has a number, the PID.

10
00:00:48,468 --> 00:00:50,124
Two columns matter here:

11
00:00:50,144 --> 00:00:54,664
percent CPU, how busy it keeps the processor, and percent memory,

12
00:00:54,684 --> 00:00:56,690
how much of the RAM it holds.

13
00:01:09,160 --> 00:01:11,730
To stop a process we send it a signal.

14
00:01:11,750 --> 00:01:16,093
The default signal, number 15, called TERM, is a polite request:

15
00:01:16,113 --> 00:01:20,660
please finish and exit. A well-behaved program cleans up and quits.

16
00:01:25,130 --> 00:01:28,289
Signal number 9, called KILL, cannot be refused.

17
00:01:28,309 --> 00:01:32,065
The kernel removes the process at once, with no clean up.

18
00:01:32,085 --> 00:01:34,582
Use it only when the polite way fails.

19
00:01:40,102 --> 00:01:44,980
First we need some trouble. The file memhog.py is a tiny Python script.

20
00:01:44,999 --> 00:01:49,533
It fills about three hundred megabytes of memory, and then sleeps.

21
00:01:55,003 --> 00:01:59,209
The command yes prints the letter y again and again, without end,

22
00:01:59,229 --> 00:02:01,939
so it keeps one processor core fully busy.

23
00:02:01,959 --> 00:02:05,320
The greater-than sign sends its output to /dev/null,

24
00:02:05,340 --> 00:02:07,661
the bin that throws everything away.

25
00:02:07,681 --> 00:02:12,732
The ampersand at the end runs it in the background, so we get our prompt back.

26
00:02:29,202 --> 00:02:32,425
We start a second yes, and the memory script, the same way.

27
00:02:40,945 --> 00:02:45,081
Now we look. ps lists processes. The letters a, u, x mean:

28
00:02:45,101 --> 00:02:50,312
all users, a user-friendly format, and also processes without a terminal.

29
00:02:56,782 --> 00:03:03,093
The option --sort=-%cpu sorts by CPU. The minus sign means biggest first.

30
00:03:03,113 --> 00:03:09,946
The pipe sends the list to head, and head -n 4 keeps only the first four lines.

31
00:03:18,416 --> 00:03:23,785
There they are: two yes processes, each using almost one hundred percent of a core.

32
00:03:23,805 --> 00:03:25,862
The PID is in the second column.

33
00:03:33,382 --> 00:03:35,064
top shows the same live.

34
00:03:35,084 --> 00:03:39,744
We use -b for batch mode, plain text, and -n 1 to print just once.

35
00:03:39,764 --> 00:03:43,218
Normally you type only top, then press q to quit.

36
00:03:52,688 --> 00:03:55,824
The top lines show the load and the memory.

37
00:03:55,844 --> 00:03:59,493
The table below lists the busiest processes first.

38
00:03:59,513 --> 00:04:04,337
Inside top, capital P sorts by CPU, and capital M sorts by memory.

39
00:04:15,857 --> 00:04:19,831
For memory we sort by percent memory instead. At the top:

40
00:04:19,851 --> 00:04:24,316
python3 memhog.py, holding ten and a half percent of the memory,

41
00:04:24,336 --> 00:04:26,418
about three hundred megabytes.

42
00:04:33,888 --> 00:04:37,801
With -e and -o you can choose the columns yourself.

43
00:04:37,821 --> 00:04:44,279
-e means every process, and -o lists the columns: PID, command name, CPU and memory.

44
00:04:54,799 --> 00:05:00,693
Now we stop them. pgrep finds processes by name, and -x means the exact name.

45
00:05:00,713 --> 00:05:02,459
It prints the two PIDs.

46
00:05:08,929 --> 00:05:14,879
kill followed by a PID sends the polite signal 15. No output means it worked.

47
00:05:14,899 --> 00:05:21,005
ps -p asks about one PID, and it shows only the header, so the process is gone.

48
00:05:31,525 --> 00:05:36,244
pkill works by name instead of number. It stops every process called yes.

49
00:05:36,264 --> 00:05:39,036
And pgrep finds none, so they are all gone.

50
00:05:45,506 --> 00:05:48,050
The memory script gets the hard way.

51
00:05:48,070 --> 00:05:51,896
kill -9 sends signal nine, and ps -p shows it is gone.

52
00:05:51,916 --> 00:05:54,531
We could have tried plain kill first.

53
00:06:02,051 --> 00:06:05,487
Our five steps: task, demo, check, reboot, and your turn.

54
00:06:07,007 --> 00:06:11,851
One more fact. We start a sleep process, and find it with pgrep. Then we reboot.

55
00:06:18,321 --> 00:06:20,763
After the reboot, pgrep finds nothing.

56
00:06:20,783 --> 00:06:25,298
A reboot ends every process, and uptime says the machine just started.

57
00:06:32,818 --> 00:06:38,446
Your turn. Make a folder called lab twenty-three. Start yes in the background.

58
00:06:38,466 --> 00:06:42,645
Find its PID with ps, and save the PID in a file, pid.txt.

59
00:06:42,665 --> 00:06:45,107
Then kill it, and run the checker.

60
00:06:51,577 --> 00:06:53,328
Try before you open the solution.

61
00:06:58,848 --> 00:07:01,132
Next up: adjust process scheduling.

62
00:07:01,602 --> 00:07:04,505
Subscribe to follow the series, and let's keep going.
